without rollback), then the deploy is not the root cause \u2014 a transient dependency issue is.\"\n- This is testable: wait for dependency recovery\n- Falsifiable: if error rate doesn't recover, this fails\n\nOption 3: \"If the error rate is identical in the canary deployment region (where the deploy hasn't been pushed), then the deploy is not the root cause \u2014 the issue is external.\"\n- This is testable: check canary region\n- Falsifiable: if canary shows different error rate, this fails\n\nI think Option 1 or 3 are the strongest. Let me go with Option 1 as it's more general and addresses the core issue.\n\nActually, let me refine this further. The hypothesis should be specific and testable:\n\n\"If the error rate correlates with external traffic patterns (not deploy timing), then the deploy is not the root cause.\"\n\nThis is:\n\n\n- Specific: correlates with traffic patterns\n- Testable: can measure correlation\n- Falsifiable: if they don't correlate, hypothesis fails\n\nThe core insight is that temporal coincidence doesn't establish causation. I need to distinguish between correlation (timing matches) and actual causation (deploy introduced the error). The testable prediction is that if errors track traffic volume rather than deploy timing, then something external is driving the spike.", "time": {"start": 1788450339747, "end": 1788450342103}, "id": "prt_067f27ba3001wa6HKYPU75gFlx", "sessionID": "ses_fa171f54fffey4ag9ll8OcnfQz", "messageID": "msg_067f270120019umrg280ozUFZk"}, {"type": "text", "text": "If the error rate correlates with traffic volume (not deploy timing), then the deploy is not the root cause.", "time": {"start": 1788450342105, "end": 1788450342299}, "id": "prt_067f284d9001fsNj6JvkLN7UcO", "sessionID": "ses_fa171f54fffey4ag9ll8OcnfQz", "messageID": "msg_067f270120019umrg280ozUFZk"}, {"reason": "stop", "type": "step-finish", "tokens": {"total": 36668, "input": 532, "output": 552, "reasoning": 0, "cache": {"write": 0, "read": 35584}}, "cost": 0